course: biology
lesson: 291
title: Bees, worms and soil: the hidden helpers of our food
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Watch a bee on an apple blossom: it is after nectar, but dusty pollen sticks to its hairy body and is carried to the next flower. Without that delivery the blossom would never become an apple.
explain: Under your feet another team is working. Earthworms pull dead leaves down, eat them with soil and leave rich worm casts, while their tunnels let air and rain sink in.
explain: A single handful of healthy soil holds more living things than there are people on Earth: bacteria, fungi, tiny animals and roots, all turning dead stuff into food for plants.
explain: Farmers have other helpers too: ladybirds that eat aphids and barn owls that hunt mice. When people forget these helpers, things go wrong, as in the 1930s Dust Bowl, when bare ploughed soil simply blew away.
fact: Bees carry pollen from flower to flower so that fruit can form, and about one in every three mouthfuls we eat depends on pollinators such as bees.
fact: Apples, almonds, strawberries and coffee all need pollinators.
fact: Honeybees are kept by beekeepers and are not close to dying out, but many wild bees and other pollinators are declining because of lost flowers, pesticides, disease and climate change.
fact: Wild bees such as bumblebees do a large share of pollination.
fact: Honey is made by many worker bees from the nectar they collect; it takes thousands of flower visits and weeks of work to fill a jar.
fact: In parts of Sichuan in China, after heavy pesticide use and the loss of bees, workers pollinated pear trees by hand with brushes, which is slow and costly.
fact: Every spring about two thirds of America's managed honeybee hives are carried on lorries to pollinate California's almond farms, one of the biggest pollination events in the world.
fact: Earthworms eat dead leaves and soil and leave rich casts, their tunnels let air and water into the soil, and Charles Darwin wrote a whole book about worms.
fact: Cutting a worm in half usually kills it; sometimes the head end heals and survives, but the tail end cannot grow a new head, and it is cruel to try.
fact: A handful of healthy soil holds more living things than there are people on Earth, and it takes hundreds of years to make a few centimetres of soil.
fact: Compost is old food and plants broken down by microbes, fungi and worms into rich soil, and it gets warm in the middle as the microbes work.
fact: Ladybirds eat aphids, tiny insects that suck plant juice, so farmers use them and other helpers instead of sprays; this is natural pest control.
fact: A barn owl family can eat thousands of mice and voles in a year, so farmers put up owl boxes.
fact: In the 1930s the ploughed prairies of America dried out and the soil blew away in huge dust storms called the Dust Bowl; many families lost their farms, and farmers learned to plant trees and keep soil covered.
exercise: How does a bee help an apple tree? | It carries pollen from flower to flower so fruit can form | It eats the green pests that chew holes in the apple leaves | It waters the blossom with drops of nectar so it can grow | It makes the apples sweeter by dripping honey on them
exercise: About how much of our food depends on pollinators? | About one mouthful in three | Almost none | All of it | One mouthful in a thousand
exercise: Which bees are really declining? | Many wild bees, like some bumblebees | Honeybees kept by beekeepers are almost extinct | No bees at all are in any kind of trouble | Only the bees that live in big cities
exercise: Which of these is a cause of bees and other pollinators declining? | Lost flowers and pesticides | Too many gardens full of flowers | Cold winters and nothing else | Bees eating too much of their own honey
exercise: What did workers do in parts of Sichuan after the bees were lost? | Pollinated pear trees by hand with brushes | Planted plastic flowers that smell of nectar | Gave up growing fruit there for ever | Trained small birds to carry the pollen
exercise: How do bees get to California's almond farms? | Their hives are driven there on lorries | They fly all the way there from Canada | They are born inside the almond trees | They come across the sea by ship from China
exercise: What do earthworm tunnels do for soil? | Let air and water in | Make the soil dry and hard | Carry the soil away to the river | Keep plant roots from growing down
exercise: Which famous scientist wrote a whole book about worms? | Charles Darwin | Isaac Newton | Albert Einstein | Marie Curie
exercise: What usually happens if a worm is cut in half? | It usually dies, though the head end sometimes survives | It always becomes two whole worms that both crawl away | It grows a second head on the cut end | Nothing happens, because worms feel nothing
exercise: How many living things are in a handful of healthy soil? | More than there are people on Earth | About ten, mostly worms and beetles | None, because soil is just dead dirt | Exactly a hundred, no more and no less
exercise: How long does it take to make a few centimetres of soil? | Hundreds of years | One rainy week in spring | One long hot summer | A single day after rain
exercise: What turns kitchen scraps into compost? | Microbes, fungi and worms | Sunlight and nothing else at all | Salt and vinegar | Plastic bags that hold in the heat
exercise: Why does a compost heap feel warm inside? | Microbes give off heat as they work | Someone hid a little heater inside it | The sun shines right through it | The worms rub together and light small fires
exercise: Why do farmers welcome ladybirds? | They eat aphids that suck plant juice | They pollinate the wheat in the fields | They scare the mice away from the barn | They make the soil richer with their droppings
exercise: Why might a farmer put up a barn owl box? | Owls eat thousands of mice and voles | Owls lay big eggs that the farmer can sell | Owls guard the hens from foxes at night | Owls eat the weeds
exercise: What was the Dust Bowl? | Dust storms in the 1930s when ploughed, dried prairie soil blew away | A giant bowl that cowboys used for feeding all their cattle | A desert sports game played with a ball of dust | A volcano in America that covered farms with grey ash
exercise: What did farmers learn from the Dust Bowl? | Plant trees and keep soil covered | Plough the fields more often in dry years | Grow only one crop, year after year | Burn the grass each spring
test: «bees are all dying, honey's gonna run out» What is more accurate? | Honeybees are kept by beekeepers, but many wild bees are declining | Every kind of bee will be gone next year, so honey will run out then | No bees are in trouble at all, it's all made up | Honey comes from wasps now, not bees
test: «chopped a worm with the spade, now there's two, cool» What is right? | Usually it dies; the tail end cannot grow a new head | Yes, both halves just grow into two worms | It turns into a snake | Worms grow back from any piece, even a tiny one
test: Count them: a ladybird has 6 legs. 7 ladybirds sit on a rose. How many legs? | 42 | 13 | 36 | 49
test: Count them: a barn owl family is 2 parents and 4 owlets. How many wings? | 12 | 6 | 8 | 24
test: What would happen to an orchard if every bee and pollinator vanished? | Very little fruit would form unless people pollinated by hand | The trees would make more fruit, since no bees would steal the nectar | The apples would grow on the roots instead | Nothing at all, because the wind does all of it
test: In a cartoon a happy bee makes honey from a flower in one minute and fills a jar. What is real? | Honey takes many bees many flights and weeks of work | One bee really can fill a whole jar in a minute | Bees make honey by chewing up green leaves | Bees buy their honey from people
test: In Winnie the Pooh, Pooh scoops honey from a tree with his paw. Who really made that honey? | Many worker bees, from nectar they collected | Pooh himself, by licking flowers | The tree itself, from the sweet sap inside its trunk | One queen bee all on her own
test: «compost heap's steaming, is it on fire» What is happening? | Microbes breaking down the scraps make heat | Yes, it is about to burst into flames | Someone poured a kettle of hot water in | The worms are cooking it
test: Why could spraying a field against aphids end up making the aphid problem worse? | The spray may kill the ladybirds that eat the aphids | Sprays make the aphids grow bigger and hungrier | Aphids love the smell of the spray and fly in from miles around | Ladybirds eat the spray and grow
test: «dirt's just dirt, mate» What would a soil scientist say? | A handful holds more living things than there are people on Earth | Soil is crushed stone and nothing else | Only worms live in soil, and nothing smaller than a worm | Soil has no life in it at all during the winter months
test: What would happen if a farmer left a huge ploughed field bare through a hot windy drought? | The dry soil could blow away like in the Dust Bowl | The soil would grow a crop alone | The wind would make the soil richer | Worms would cover it in a night
test: Why is it so serious to lose soil, when it is just under our feet? | A few centimetres take hundreds of years to form | Soil can be made in a factory in a single day | We can grow all our food on bare rock instead | Soil grows back by itself every spring
exam: Identify the principal service rendered by the honeybee to the cultivation of fruit. | The transfer of pollen between flowers, permitting the formation of fruit | The destruction of insects injurious to the leaves of fruit trees | The irrigation of blossoms by means of nectar | The enrichment of the soil of orchards by means of honey and wax
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bisection of an earthworm. | It usually results in death, the posterior part being incapable of forming a new head | It invariably produces two complete worms, each of which regenerates the missing part | It causes the worm to grow two heads | It is without consequence for the animal, which feels no pain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Dust Bowl of the 1930s? | The erosion by wind of dried, ploughed prairie soil, resulting in the loss of many farms | A period of abundant harvests upon the American prairies, owing to deep and frequent ploughing | A volcanic eruption in the western United States | A flood which deposited great quantities of dust upon the plains
exam: Ascertain the role of the ladybird in agriculture. | It consumes aphids and thereby serves as a natural means of pest control | It pollinates cereal crops and is therefore indispensable to the harvest | It consumes stored grain | It enriches the soil with its casts in the manner of the earthworm
